WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
REST APIs.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:03.621
REST isn't a library.

3
00:00:03.621 --> 00:00:09.833
It's a design discipline, and once clients depend on your endpoints, your mistakes ship to them forever.

4
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:12.700
REST rests on three habits.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.900
Name resources with nouns, like missions slash forty two slash logs, never get mission logs.

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.600
Use HTTP verbs as the actions.

7
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.067
And return correct status codes so clients can react programmatically.

8
00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:30.800
The deepest idea is idempotency: is retrying safe?

9
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.262
Get, put and delete are idempotent.

10
00:00:33.262 --> 00:00:35.733
Doing them twice equals doing them once.

11
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:36.882
Post is not.

12
00:00:36.882 --> 00:00:40.700
Retry a timed out post and you may create a duplicate.

13
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.033
Every retry policy leans on this.

14
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:46.233
Here's the trap and the standard fix.

15
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:48.300
A client posts to create a log.

16
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.833
The reply times out, so the client can't tell if it worked.

17
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.033
It retries, sending the same idempotency key.

18
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:59.133
The server recognizes the key, and creates the log only once.

19
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:03.467
Here's a mission log service, designed rather than accreted.

20
00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:06.711
Get returns a filtered list with a two hundred.

21
00:01:06.711 --> 00:01:09.467
Post creates a log and returns a two oh one.

22
00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:13.200
Put replaces, patch edits part of it, and delete removes it.

23
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.633
And an idempotency key header lets the server dedupe retried posts.

24
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:20.633
Here's why design is permanent.

25
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:28.033
You expose your exoplanet classifier as post slash predictions, returning a two oh one with the result's URL.

26
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:31.133
Six months later, three teams' dashboards call it.

27
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:33.425
A colleague suggests renaming it.

28
00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:40.667
Now you can push back: that's a breaking change, so it needs a version two, deprecation headers, and a migration window.

29
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:46.833
The classic mistake is putting verbs in the URL, and ignoring status codes.

30
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:51.533
The fix: nouns for resources, verbs as actions, and correct codes.

31
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:53.597
Let's recap.

32
00:01:53.597 --> 00:01:56.333
Nouns for resources, verbs as actions.

33
00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:58.467
Return correct status codes.

34
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:01.199
Get, put and delete are idempotent.

35
00:02:01.199 --> 00:02:02.033
Post is not.

36
00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:04.688
And shipped contracts are permanent.

37
00:02:04.688 --> 00:02:05.800
Next up: GraphQL.
